# Lint and format what is staged — `eslint --fix` then `prettier --write`, per
# file, in that order. See the //lint-staged note in the root package.json for
# the pattern shapes and why there are three of them.
bunx lint-staged

# Then lint and the unit suites, for the packages this commit moves and the
# packages that depend on them. The full side-scoped suites — end-to-end runs
# included — moved to .husky/pre-push on 2026-08-07. Two tiers:
#
#   pre-commit   lint + unit, the changed packages and their dependents
#   pre-push     lint + unit + end-to-end, the whole side that moved
#
# ESLint runs in BOTH places since 2026-08-08, and that is not a duplication to
# tidy away. It rejoined lint-staged above because the reason it had been
# excluded expired: ESLint 9 looked a flat config up from the working directory
# and its ancestors, lint-staged runs its commands from the git root, and this
# repository has no eslint.config at its root, so the entry failed on every
# commit staging a .ts file. ESLint 10 looks the config up from the directory of
# the FILE being linted instead. Verified by running it: from the root,
# `eslint packages/cli/src/cli/base-command.ts` now exits 0 having loaded
# packages/cli/eslint.config.js, projectService carve-out and all — under 9 the
# same command exited 2. That file is the verification rather than a sample: it
# carries an eslint-disable for a type-aware rule and packages/cli reports
# unused disable directives as errors, so a per-file run in which the type-aware
# rules had not actually loaded would have failed on the directive. There is
# still no root fallback; `eslint vitest.config.mjs` at the root exits 2 because
# nothing above it holds a config.
#
# What keeps ESLint on this line as well is a rule inventory, not a mechanism.
# Twenty-four of the rules enabled in all seven workspaces are type-aware, and a
# type-aware verdict reads a whole TypeScript program: a change in a staged file
# can produce a report in a file the commit does not touch, and no staged-file
# run can see that one. Nothing else in the seven configs needs more than the
# file it is handed — the config-gate import bans are no-restricted-imports,
# which matches the literal specifier string and never resolves a module, the
# task-ID and dynamic-import bans are no-restricted-syntax selectors, and no
# import-cycle plugin exists anywhere here. So lint-staged is the fast half and
# this is the complete one. The cost of the complete half is bounded —
# packages/cli lints in ~19s and apps/editor in ~5s, both under type-aware
# rules, and turbo caches both.

# The staged list answers one question now: did a manifest move. It used to
# answer "which side moved" as well, which is why it is shaped the way it is —
# no --diff-filter, because a commit that only deletes a package.json changes
# the answer too, and --no-renames, which splits a move into both of its paths.
staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only --no-renames)

# The four cross-workspace checks read every package.json, every tsconfig.json,
# every vitest.config.* and every eslint.config.*, so those are exactly the files
# that can change their answer — anywhere in the tree, which is why this list is
# not anchored. The checks compare workspaces to each other: a commit that
# touches only the web side can still make the CLI's tsconfig the odd one out.
# `bun run deps:check` measures at 0.2s, so there is nothing to scope more finely
# than this.
#
# The grep sits inside an `if` on purpose: husky runs this with `sh -e`, so a
# bare grep that matched nothing would fail the commit on its own.
run_deps=no

if printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -qE '(^|/)(package\.json|bun\.lock|tsconfig[^/]*\.json|vitest\.config\.[^/]+|eslint\.config\.[^/]+)$'; then
	run_deps=yes
fi

if [ "$run_deps" = yes ]; then
	bun run deps:check
fi

# `...[HEAD]` is turbo's changed-package filter, widened by the leading `...` to
# every package that depends on one that changed.
#
# This hook used to refuse it, and the refusal was earned: a commit to
# packages/matrix once selected matrix and apps/editor but not apps/server,
# which depends on matrix too, so a change that broke the worker committed
# clean. The note here ended "please do not narrow it again". Scoping by side
# instead — the whole CLI suite if the CLI moved, the whole web suite if the web
# moved — cannot miss a dependent, because it never tries to work out what the
# dependents are.
#
# What changed on 2026-08-07 is not the filter but what stands behind it. Those
# side-scoped suites still run in full; they run at pre-push, before any of this
# leaves the machine. A dependent this filter misses now costs an amended commit
# rather than a broken worker on main, and that is the whole trade: this hook is
# allowed to be fast and occasionally wrong because the push hook is slow and
# cannot be. Narrowing pre-push is the move that would hurt.
#
# What [HEAD] compares against, since a hook is the one place it matters. Turbo
# reads a one-ended range as "since this ref, including work not yet committed",
# and asks git three things: diff-tree from HEAD to HEAD, which is empty;
# `ls-files --others --modified`, the untracked and the unstaged; and
# `git diff --name-only --cached`, the staged set this commit is made of. The
# commit's own contents are therefore always in the selection, along with
# anything else left dirty in the tree — a superset, never a subset. Read out of
# turbo 2.10.8, crates/turborepo-scm/src/git.rs; the published docs do not say.
#
# Files belonging to no workspace — root package.json, turbo.json, bun.lock,
# .husky, .github, docs — make turbo answer "then every package changed", so the
# root-tooling clause the old hook spelled out is now turbo's, and wider.
#
# test:e2e is deliberately absent: it is the slowest thing either side owns, and
# pre-push runs it.
#
# `typecheck` joined this line on 2026-08-08 (REPO-33). Two reference docs had
# been claiming for months that TypeScript is checked on every commit, and no
# hook had ever run it; the ruling was to make the claim true rather than to
# correct it. The task order below does not matter — turbo builds its graph from
# turbo.json's dependsOn, not from argv, and these three are independent of each
# other. Each carries `^build`, so a cold checkout builds dependencies first.
# The CLI's `typecheck` is three tsc programs, and the middle one is
# tsconfig.scripts.json — which is what puts scripts/ under a commit-time gate
# for the first time.
bunx turbo run lint typecheck test --filter='...[HEAD]'

# The D1 migrations, and it is on the SAME filter as the line above rather than
# unconditional: apps/server is selected only when it or something it depends on
# moved, which is exactly when a schema edit could have arrived without its
# migration. `wrangler deploy` ships code and not schema, so the alternative to
# catching it here is catching it at the edge, where the code expects a column
# nothing added. It is a separate line because it is a separate task — folding it
# into `test` would put a drizzle-kit spawn inside the suite that runs most
# often, which is how a check gets skipped.
bunx turbo run db:generate:check --filter='...[HEAD]'
